# Phase 2 — Audio and stream interaction

## Problem

Phase 1 delivered the multi-turn agentic loop, the single Chat-Completions
adapter with prepare-time capability probes, the configured delivery list,
`chat.read`/`users.read`/`screen.capture`/`chat.write`, the PC agent with its
WebSocket JSON proxy and the two deployment profiles (`main` at `7e26038`,
1363 passed). The companion can read and look, but it cannot listen, cannot
speak, cannot change what the stream shows and cannot ask the audience a
question through the platform.

Phase 2 completes the seven starter units of design-v2 §4.4 with the three
remaining modules — `audio_output`, `audio_input`, `stream_control` — behind
the phase 0/1 contracts: explicit terminal outcomes, default-deny
authorization, bounded media on the run-leased store, the same executor for
local providers and the proxy, and a capability view that reports what is
actually ready. It adds the `audio` capability to the single model adapter,
verified like structured output and vision, and keeps the agentic loop
untouched: audio output enters as delivery entries, exactly as phase 1's
delivery list was designed for.

Authority: `docs/design-v2.md` §2.4, §3.3, §3.4, §4.3, §4.4 and §6 "Phase 2";
`docs/proxy-protocol.md` (protocol v1, to be conformed to); the phase 1 spec
and plan under `docs/campaigns/phase1/`. The root `spec.md`/`plan.md` are the
historical v1 documents and are not modified.

### Environment facts this spec is honest about

- A speech synthesis/transcription HTTP service listens on loopback port
  5050 without authentication on the reference machine. It is one candidate
  provider; every endpoint, model and voice stays a module setting.
- Playback and recording tooling exists on the reference machine as ordinary
  executables; the modules drive a configured command, never a named tool.
- No streaming-software scene provider is installed and its port is closed:
  `stream_control` must stay discovered-but-not-ready for scenes on this
  machine, the boundary being proven against a scripted provider.

### Decisions this spec settles (design §6 delegated them to implementation)

Each is a contract, not a mechanism; the plan chooses the code.

1. **Write actions stay out of the model's tools.** Phase 1's read-only
   proposal restriction is unchanged (design §3.1: the initial vertical
   allows no intermediate write). The four new write actions are reachable
   (a) as delivery entries when they declare the delivery capability
   (`audio.speak` with text, `audio.play` and `stream.scene.set` effect-only)
   and (b) through the executor by any principal holding an applicable rule
   (tests exercise the contracts this way). `stream.poll.create` declares no
   delivery capability: creating a poll is not delivering an answer. Letting
   the model propose policy-permitted writes is a phase 3+ decision.
2. **Speech synthesis happens in the module that serves `audio.speak`.** The
   provider is an HTTP endpoint (loopback service or remote service) taking
   the speech request shape of the API family the model adapter already
   speaks (design §3.1); playback happens on the machine running the module.
   In the server profile the action is proxy-bound, so synthesis and
   playback both happen on the agent, whose synthesis endpoint may itself be
   remote. No audio bytes therefore travel brain → agent, and protocol v1
   needs no new frame or direction.
3. **`audio.play` plays configured clips.** Its audio reference is the name
   of a clip configured on the output machine; playing a run attachment is
   not in phase 2 (it would need a brain → agent transfer). This keeps the
   action usable as an effect-only delivery entry (a chime) and as an
   executor action.
4. **Playback completed is the success condition.** `audio.speak` and
   `audio.play` end `success` only when the configured player has consumed
   the whole segment and terminated normally before the call deadline; a
   created synthesis that was not played is never `success`. Playback state
   is local, so these actions never end `external_unknown`.
5. **Segment convention.** Captured audio is WAV, PCM 16-bit, 16 000 Hz,
   mono — 32 000 bytes per second, 44-byte header — bounded by requested
   seconds and by bytes, stored under the run's lease with content type
   `audio/wav`, returned as an `audio_ref` part carrying duration and an
   optional dated transcription. The store, the executor and the proxy treat
   it exactly as an image reference.
6. **Transcription never fails a capture.** When enabled, one bounded
   request follows the capture; its failure is reported in
   `transcription_status`, the observation stays `success` with its
   `audio_ref`. Without a transcription, an `audio_ref` reaching a run whose
   backend lacks the verified `audio` capability ends the run
   `error capability_missing`; with one, the transcription text is what the
   model receives and the omission is recorded in the run record and trace —
   explicit, never silent.
7. **Scene provider boundary.** `stream_control` talks to a scene provider
   through one interface (`list scenes`, `current scene`, `set scene`) with
   three kinds: `none` (action declared, not bound — the proxy may serve it),
   `scripted` (tests) and `websocket` (obs-websocket protocol version 5,
   the protocol the reference streaming software serves on port 4455; URL
   and password credential in settings — R6 names the handshake and the
   three requests). Confirmation is a read-back of the current scene after
   the set command. The vendor name lives in `modules/stream_control/` and
   the README only, never in `core/` or the brain.
8. **Polls go through a platform service, not a platform import.** Platform
   modules publish a poll service (`create`, `get`) in a new bounded runtime
   service registry under `(kind: poll, platform)`; `stream_control` resolves
   one per platform at prepare and binds `stream.poll.create` for
   `<platform>/*/poll` only where a service exists. Design §4.4 allows the
   starter units to share a platform provider; the core registry knows no
   kind name. State tracking is by the service's `get` and the module's
   bounded table; no platform event subscription in phase 2.
9. **One command, one effect.** A poll is created by at most one request per
   call, ever. A lost confirmation after the request was sent triggers one
   reconciliation read (`get`) within the remaining time: a matching poll
   found → `success` with `reconciled: true`; none → `external_unknown`
   (cause `confirmation_lost`), and the next create on that channel
   reconciles first. Same shape for scenes with the read-back.
10. **Optional dependencies degrade, they do not abort.** Each new module has
    a `required` setting (default `false`). With `false`, a dependency that
    is unavailable at prepare leaves that action unbound, is reported
    `module.degraded` naming the action and a value-free reason, and startup
    continues; with `true`, prepare fails naming module and dependency. The
    phase 1 `capture` module keeps its phase 1 behaviour.
11. **Remote readiness is call-time honest.** The brain's ready view lists a
    proxy-bound action as ready while an agent that declared it is paired;
    an action whose module is not ready on the agent answers
    `refused provider_not_ready` from the agent's executor. Propagating
    agent-side readiness changes over the wire is deferred and recorded as a
    limit.

### Standing constraints

- Phase 0 and phase 1 guarantees stay in force: bounded admission, phase
  lifecycle, explicit terminal outcomes (`success`/`refused`/`error`/
  `timeout`/`cancelled`/`external_unknown`), default-deny including reads,
  bounded retention, one global startup and one global shutdown deadline,
  redaction of declared credentials and configured secrets in traces and
  loss diagnostics, one action per run turn, an uncertain external outcome
  never memorised as a confirmed effect.
- No module, platform, device, streaming-software or vendor name is added to
  `core/main.py`, `core/` or the brain; every new module starts and stops
  through its manifest and `enabled_modules`.
- Runtime dependencies remain `aiohttp` and `PyYAML`.
- Tests never use a positive-duration sleep: clock, RNG, sleepers,
  subprocess runners and transports are injected (the existing whole-suite
  check applies).
- Observations, events and traces carry references and metadata only: no
  base64 payload, no local filesystem path.

### Target list and permitted scope

The `targets` list is the complete set of repository files this phase creates
or modifies; the plan's `Files:` lines are a subset of it and the review gate
compares `git diff --name-only <base>..HEAD` against it. **Permitted scope
beyond the list:** the campaign's own directory `docs/campaigns/phase2/`
(brief, this spec, the plan, README and `scaffolding/`) may change at any
step; nothing under it is runtime code, a test, a profile or packaging.

## Requirements

- R1: **Audio output: `audio.speak` and `audio.play` with playback-completed
  semantics.** `modules/audio_output/` provides two write actions on
  destinations `*/*/audio`. `audio.speak` (permission `audio.speak`; args
  `text` of 1..`max_text_chars` characters, default 400; optional `voice`
  from `voices.allowed`, default `voices.default`; optional `output` naming
  a configured entry of `outputs`, default `default_output`) synthesises
  the text through the configured provider `synthesis` (`endpoint`, `model`,
  optional `api_key` credential, `timeout_seconds` default 10, `probe`
  default `true`): one request per call, JSON body `{model, voice, input,
  response_format: "wav"}`, whose 2xx body must be a parseable RIFF/WAVE PCM
  file; then plays it on the output. `audio.play` (permission `audio.play`;
  args `sound` naming an entry of `clips` — WAV files on the output machine
  — optional `output`) plays the clip. Playback contract: an output is
  `{player: {argv[]}}`; the segment bytes are fed to the player's standard
  input in bounded writes; playback completed ⇔ the player terminated
  normally (exit 0) after accepting the whole segment, observed before the
  call deadline; then the observation is `success` with result `{output,
  duration_ms, played_ms, playback: "completed"}` plus `voice` (speak) or
  `sound` (play), `played_ms == duration_ms`, `duration_ms` computed from
  the WAV header (data bytes ÷ (sample rate × channels × bytes per
  sample)). **Progress contract:** the only progress the module can observe
  through this interface is the count of data bytes the player has accepted
  on its standard input (a blocking pipe: a write returns once the player
  has read the bytes or they sit in the pipe buffer); `played_ms` is that
  count converted with the same header formula, rounded down to the
  millisecond and capped at `duration_ms`. It is a documented estimate —
  bytes accepted by the pipe may not yet have reached the device — and the
  README and the manifest's action description say so; no device-level
  clock is claimed. The player runner seam reports the bytes it accepted,
  which is what tests measure. Failure taxonomy, each with 0 playback unless stated: `refused voice_not_allowed`
  / `output_not_allowed` (value outside the allowlist, provider uninvoked);
  `refused resource_busy` (below); `error invalid_arguments` (unknown
  `sound`); `error tts_unavailable` (transport failure), `error tts_failed`
  (non-2xx; the status code in the error, never the body), `timeout` with
  cause `synthesis` (no answer within `timeout_seconds`), `error
  invalid_audio` (unparseable or empty body), `error speech_too_long`
  (duration above `max_speech_seconds`, default 20), `error audio_too_large`
  (body above `max_audio_bytes`, default 5 242 880), `error clip_unreadable`
  (clip missing at call time), `error playback_failed` (player not
  startable or non-zero exit; `played_ms` = the accepted-bytes estimate,
  0 when never started); the call deadline
  reached during playback stops the player (terminate, then kill after
  `stop_grace_seconds`, default 1) and ends `timeout` with cause `playback`
  and the `played_ms` reached; a cancellation stops it the same way and ends
  `cancelled` with `played_ms`. These two actions never end
  `external_unknown`. **Serialization:** one playback at a time per output;
  a call arriving while the output is busy waits behind at most
  `max_waiters` (default 1) callers and starts when the previous playback
  ended; one more is `refused resource_busy` immediately. **Readiness at
  `prepare`:** for each output the player's `argv[0]` must resolve to an
  executable; with `synthesis.probe: true` one bounded probe synthesis of
  `synthesis.probe_text` (default `"ready"`) must succeed within
  `timeout_seconds`. `audio.play` is bound when at least one output is
  usable; `audio.speak` when in addition the probe succeeded — or, with
  `synthesis.probe: false`, when at least one output is usable: the
  synthesis endpoint is then unverified at `prepare`, 0 requests are sent,
  `audio.speak` binds with no `module.degraded`, `required: true` cannot
  fail on the synthesis dependency, and an unreachable endpoint surfaces on
  the first call as `error tts_unavailable` (call-time honest, decision
  11). The `required` policy of R8 applies to the outputs and, with `probe:
  true`, to the probe. The `api_key` credential is declared and redacted.
  Seams for tests: injected synthesis transport, player runner, sleeper and
  clock, read from settings never from a configuration file.

- R2: **Audio and scene actions enter the delivery list without touching the
  loop.** The manifests declare `delivery: {text_argument: text}` on
  `audio.speak` and `delivery: {text_argument: none}` on `audio.play` and
  `stream.scene.set`; `audio.capture` and `stream.poll.create` declare no
  delivery capability. Phase 1's resolution rules apply unchanged: a `fixed`
  list `[{chat.write, text}, {audio.speak, text}]` writes and speaks the
  answer, `[{chat.write, text}, {audio.play, none, arguments: {sound}}]`
  writes and plays a clip, `[{stream.scene.set, none, arguments: {scene}}]`
  performs an effect-only delivery; `mode: modules` derives the list from
  the enabled modules in `preference[]` order, each entry taking its text
  mapping from the declaration. Each entry keeps its own terminal outcome in
  `deliveries[]`, an entry whose module is not ready is `refused
  provider_not_ready` without dropping the others, and an
  `external_unknown` entry is never memorised as a confirmed send. The
  brain's delivery resolution and agentic loop code are not modified for
  this requirement.

- R3: **Audio capture: `audio.capture`, bounded segment, on demand only,
  optional dated transcription.** `modules/audio_input/` provides the read
  action `audio.capture` (permission `audio.capture`; destinations
  `*/*/audio`; args `seconds`, integer 1..`max_seconds` default 30; optional
  `source` naming a configured source, default `default_source`). Sources:
  kind `command` (configured argv writing WAV on standard output, started at
  the call, stopped at the requested duration; killed at the deadline) and
  kind `file` (a WAV re-read at each call, truncated to `seconds`). The
  module validates the segment — RIFF/WAVE, PCM, 16-bit, 16 000 Hz, mono,
  else `error invalid_result` with 0 bytes stored — truncates its data to
  `seconds × 32 000` bytes, refuses more than `max_bytes` (default
  1 048 576) with `error attachment_refused` and 0 bytes stored, and stores
  it under the call's run lease with content type `audio/wav`. Result:
  `{source, content_type: "audio/wav", size, duration_ms, sample_rate_hz:
  16000, channels: 1, captured_at, transcription_status}` plus exactly one
  `audio_ref` part (R4). A `seconds` value above the time left before the
  call deadline minus `grace_seconds` (default 2) is `error
  capture_too_long` before any process is spawned; a recorder that produced
  no valid segment by the deadline is killed and the call is `error
  capture_timed_out` with 0 bytes stored; a cancellation kills it and ends
  `cancelled` with 0 bytes stored. **Transcription** (`transcription`:
  `enabled` default `false`, `endpoint`, `model`, optional `api_key`
  credential, optional `language`, `timeout_seconds` default 10, `max_chars`
  default 2000): when enabled, exactly one request after storing (multipart
  body carrying the WAV and `model`, and `language` when set) whose 2xx JSON
  answer `{text}` becomes `audio_ref.transcription = {text, transcribed_at,
  provider_id, truncated}` with `text` cut to `max_chars` (`truncated: true`
  when cut) and `transcription_status: "ok"`; on failure the observation
  stays `success` with its `audio_ref`, `transcription` absent and
  `transcription_status ∈ {"failed:stt_unavailable", "failed:stt_failed",
  "failed:stt_timed_out", "failed:invalid_transcription",
  "skipped:deadline"}` (the last when under 1 s remains before the call
  deadline); `"disabled"` when not enabled; `"unavailable"` when the
  prepare-time probe failed (R8). **On demand only:** the module declares no
  producer role, spawns no task at start and no process outside a call.
  **Readiness at `prepare`:** each source is probed (executable resolvable /
  file readable), and with transcription enabled one bounded probe request
  with a generated 0.25 s silent segment (8 044 bytes) must succeed;
  `audio.capture` is bound when at least one source is usable, transcription
  unavailability degrades only the transcription capability; the `required`
  policy of R8 applies. Seams: injected source factory, subprocess runner,
  transcription transport, sleeper and clock.

- R4: **`audio_ref` observation part and the adapter's verified `audio`
  capability.** `ActionObservation.parts` accepts a third part type
  `audio_ref` with the eight fields `attachment_id`, `content_type`
  (`audio/wav`), `size`, `duration_ms`, `sample_rate_hz`, `channels`,
  `captured_at`, `provider_id`, and the optional `transcription` object of
  R3. The executor validates `audio_ref` parts on every terminal observation
  as it validates `image_ref` (attachment leased to the call's `run_id`,
  unexpired, `size` equal to the stored size; otherwise `error
  invalid_result` and the result is not fed to the model); the observation
  size adds each `audio_ref` `size`; every `audio_ref` of a run is released
  with its terminal record and by the residual TTL; an expired reference at
  the model turn that would use it ends the run `error attachment_expired`.
  `capabilities.required` accepts `audio` beside `structured_output` and
  `vision`; at `prepare`, before the readiness barrier and within
  `budget.model_call_seconds`, a third probe is sent when `audio` is
  required: a forced tool call carrying one audio input part of a generated
  0.25 s, 16 kHz, mono, 16-bit silent WAV (8 044 bytes); it is verified only
  by a 2xx response of the expected shape, otherwise startup fails with the
  phase 1 diagnostic form and reason ∈ the phase 1 set ∪ {`audio_rejected`};
  at most 3 probe requests per activation. At call time, in a run whose
  verified capabilities include `audio`, each `audio_ref` of the transcript
  is resolved from the store and encoded as the backend's audio input part
  (format `wav`) for the request's duration only, together with its
  transcription text when present. In a run whose verified capabilities lack
  `audio`: an `audio_ref` carrying a transcription is rendered to the model
  as its transcription text and the omission is recorded — `audio_omitted`
  counted in the run record and in `brain.run.completed` — never silently;
  an `audio_ref` without transcription ends the run `error` (failure
  `capability_missing`, `capability: audio`) without sending the request.
  When the backend reports no usage, each audio part is estimated at 10
  tokens per second of `duration_ms` (rounded up) and flagged
  `tokens_estimated: true`. No audio bytes appear in traces or events.

- R5: **Audio over the proxy conforms to protocol v1.** The `attachment`
  header's `content_type` is accepted from the shared attachment content-type
  set {`image/png`, `image/jpeg`, `audio/wav`}; every other rule of §6, §10
  and §11 is unchanged (binary bound `min(store max_object_bytes,
  max_attachment_bytes)`, three-step transfer, bounded acknowledgement, call
  identity, identifier uniqueness, no retransmission after a drop). An
  `observation` may carry `audio_ref` parts under the same acknowledged-
  reference rule as `image_ref` (§5.5): an unacknowledged `attachment_id`
  makes the call `error attachment_refused` (or `external_unknown` for a
  write not reported `refused`) and discards its uploads. The agent transfers
  the attachment of every `audio_ref` before sending the observation and
  returns `error attachment_refused` when a transfer is refused. `v` stays
  1; `docs/proxy-protocol.md` records the addendum. A forwarded
  `audio.speak`, `audio.play` or `stream.scene.set` dropped after emission
  resolves `external_unknown` (cause `proxy_disconnected`) exactly as any
  write. The phase 2 scenario (AC35) runs across the in-memory
  transport boundary with the same contract and no capture, audio or
  transport branch in the brain.

- R6: **Scene control: allowed scene → confirmed state, serialized, with
  reconciliation.** `stream.scene.set` (write; permission `stream.scene`;
  destinations `*/*/stream`; args `scene`, 1..64 characters) is served by the
  scene provider configured in `scenes` (`provider.kind ∈ {none, scripted,
  websocket}`; `allowed[]`, 1..64 names; for `websocket`: `url` — `ws://` on
  loopback only, else `wss://`, refused at validation otherwise — the
  `password` credential, `connect_timeout_seconds` default 5,
  `request_timeout_seconds` default 5). **Wire protocol of the `websocket`
  kind:** obs-websocket protocol version 5 (RPC version 1), restricted to
  what the interface needs: the `Hello` → `Identify` → `Identified`
  handshake with `rpcVersion: 1` and `eventSubscriptions: 0`; when `Hello`
  carries an `authentication` object, `Identify` carries the authentication
  string derived from the password, the salt and the challenge as that
  protocol defines (secret = base64(sha256(password + salt)), string =
  base64(sha256(secret + challenge))); the three operations are
  `Request`/`RequestResponse` pairs matched by `requestId`: `GetSceneList`
  (list scenes; the `prepare` reachability probe issues exactly this one),
  `GetCurrentProgramScene` (current scene), `SetCurrentProgramScene
  {sceneName}` (set scene); no event subscription and no other request
  type. A handshake not reaching `Identified` within
  `connect_timeout_seconds`, a socket closed with close code 4009
  (authentication failed) or a `negotiatedRpcVersion` other than 1 is an
  unreachable provider at `prepare` and a disconnection afterwards; a
  `RequestResponse` whose `requestStatus.result` is `false` with code 600
  (resource not found) is the "scene unknown" answer; any other
  unsuccessful status on the set command is `error scene_not_applied`
  carrying the numeric code and never the `comment` text; a
  `RequestResponse` missing within `request_timeout_seconds` is the "no
  answer" case below. Contract: a `scene` outside
  `allowed` is `refused scene_not_allowed` with the provider uninvoked; the
  provider reporting the scene unknown is `error scene_unknown` with 0
  effect; after the set command the module reads back the current scene:
  equal → `success {scene, previous_scene, confirmed_at, reconciled:
  false}`; different → `error scene_not_applied`; no answer to the set
  command or a disconnection before the read-back → exactly one
  reconciliation read within the remaining time: equal → `success` with
  `reconciled: true`, otherwise `external_unknown` (cause
  `confirmation_lost`), never a second set command in the same call. A
  provider that is disconnected at call time answers `error
  provider_unavailable` and the module withdraws the action's readiness
  (`module.degraded`) until a supervised reconnection succeeds — backoff
  initial 1 s, multiplier 2, cap 30 s, full jitter from the injected RNG,
  `module.ready` on success. **Serialization:** one set command at a time
  per provider; at most `max_waiters` (default 1) waiting calls; one more is
  `refused resource_busy`. **Shutdown:** during drain, waiting calls end
  `cancelled`; a set command already sent waits for its read-back within the
  drain deadline, else `external_unknown`. With `provider.kind: none` the
  action is declared and not bound, so a proxy may serve it; with
  `websocket` and an unreachable URL at prepare the action stays unbound
  under the `required` policy of R8. The `password` credential is declared
  and redacted.

- R7: **Polls: one creation per call, tracked state, reconciliation, per-
  channel exclusion, through a platform-published service.**
  `RuntimeContext` gains the optional `services` collaborator: a bounded
  registry (at most 64 entries) where a module publishes a service under
  `(kind, platform)` during activation through its module-scoped facade and
  any module resolves one at prepare; a second publication of the same key
  fails activation naming both modules; the core defines no kind. The
  platform module publishes its poll service under `(poll, twitch)` —
  `create(channel_id, question, options, duration_seconds)` and
  `get(channel_id)` over the platform's poll operations with its existing
  credential — and the fixture platform a scripted one under `(poll, fake)`.
  `stream.poll.create` (write; permission `stream.poll`; destinations
  `<platform>/*/poll` for each platform whose service resolved; args
  `question` 1..60 characters, `options` 2..5 distinct strings of 1..25
  characters, `duration_seconds` 15..1800; bounds configurable in `polls`
  and refused as `error invalid_arguments` outside them) issues **at most one
  create request per call, ever**. Result `success {poll_id, state:
  "active", question, options[], started_at, ends_at, reconciled}`. A
  channel whose tracked table (bounded below) holds an active poll is
  `refused poll_active` with 0 requests; a channel marked uncertain by an earlier
  `external_unknown` first reconciles through `get`: an active poll found →
  `refused poll_active`, none → the create proceeds. Platform answers: 401/
  403 → `refused platform_forbidden`; other 4xx → `error poll_rejected`
  (status code and sanitised message, never the body); transport failure
  before the request was sent → `error platform_unavailable` with 0 effect;
  a 5xx (the request was sent, so the effect is uncertain) → one
  reconciliation `get`: a matching poll (same rule as below) → `success`
  with `reconciled: true`; `get` listing none → `error
  platform_unavailable` carrying the status code and never the body, the
  channel not marked; `get` failing → `external_unknown` (cause
  `confirmation_lost`) and the channel marked uncertain; no answer within
  the remaining time, connection lost after the request was sent or a
  malformed 2xx → one reconciliation `get`: a poll with the same question
  and options started at or after the call's start → `success` with
  `reconciled: true`; none, or `get` failing → `external_unknown` (cause
  `confirmation_lost`) and the channel marked uncertain for
  `duration_seconds`. **Serialization:** one create in flight per
  `(platform, channel_id)`; at most `max_waiters` (default 1) waiting;
  beyond → `refused resource_busy`; two concurrent sessions on one channel
  are serialized and the second sees `poll_active`. **Retention bound:**
  the tracked table holds at most `polls.max_tracked_channels` (default
  256) channels across all platforms and at most 32 entries per channel;
  every entry (active poll or uncertain marker) carries TTL =
  `duration_seconds + 300` s on the injected clock; expired entries, and
  channels left with no entry, are dropped at every access; when the
  channel cap is reached and no channel is reclaimable, a create on an
  untracked channel is `refused resource_busy` with 0 requests, and so is
  the 33rd live entry of a channel — a live entry is never evicted by
  pressure, so an uncertain marker cannot be forgotten before its TTL
  (standing constraint: an uncertain outcome is never memorised as
  confirmed, nor silently dropped). The action is bound for
  no platform when `polls.enabled` is `false` or no service resolved (R8
  policy). State is refreshed only by `get` at call time and by the table's
  TTL; no platform event subscription in phase 2.

- R8: **Honest enable/disable of the seven starter units, degraded startup,
  chat-only profile.** The seven units — `chat_context`, `users`, `capture`,
  `audio_output`, `audio_input`, `stream_control` and platform-provided
  `chat.write` — are enabled and disabled by `enabled_modules` alone. Each
  of the three new modules has a `required` setting (default `false`): with
  `false`, every dependency probe at `prepare` is bounded by its own timeout
  inside the global startup deadline, an unavailable dependency leaves the
  affected action(s) unbound, the module reports `module.degraded` with
  `capabilities` = the unbound action names and a value-free reason (no
  URL, no path, no credential), is marked ready only if at least one of its
  actions is bound, and startup continues; with `true`, `prepare` fails with
  a diagnostic naming the module and the dependency field and startup
  aborts. The registry's registered-ready view and the authorized view
  offered to the model exclude every unbound action, while the discovered
  view still lists every declared action of every valid manifest. A call to
  an unbound action is `refused provider_not_ready` with 0 provider
  invocations. The PC profile with `enabled_modules` reduced to the phase 1
  six (`twitch`, `chat_context`, `users`, `capture`, `brain`, `audit`) — the
  chat-only profile — passes `--check-config`, reaches readiness and runs
  the phase 1 scenario unchanged; the full PC profile with every phase 2
  endpoint pointed at a closed loopback port and no poll service credentials
  reaches readiness in degraded state with the three `module.degraded`
  traces and runs the chat scenario. Remote readiness follows decision 11.

- R9: **Profiles, packaging, protocol addendum and documented per-provider
  trials.** `config.yaml.example` enables, in this order, `twitch`,
  `chat_context`, `users`, `capture`, `audio_input`, `audio_output`,
  `stream_control`, `brain`, `audit` with `required: false`, endpoints and
  credentials by `${NAME}` reference, the acted budgets of phase 1
  unchanged, the single fixed delivery entry `chat.write` (an `audio.speak`
  entry present as a comment), and explicit grants for exactly its
  provided-action set {`chat.read`, `users.read`, `screen.capture`,
  `audio.capture`, `audio.speak`, `audio.play`, `stream.scene.set`,
  `stream.poll.create`, `chat.write`}. `config.server.yaml.example` enables
  `twitch`, `chat_context`, `users`, `stream_control` (scenes `none`, polls
  enabled), `brain`, `audit`, `proxy` with `actions: [screen.capture,
  audio.capture, audio.speak, audio.play, stream.scene.set]` and grants for
  its provided-action set (the nine names above, five from the proxy
  allowlist). `agent.yaml.example` enables `capture`, `audio_input`,
  `audio_output`, `stream_control` (scenes `websocket`, polls disabled),
  `agent_link` with the same five actions and an agent-side rule granting
  each to principal `brain`; provided-action set {`screen.capture`,
  `audio.capture`, `audio.speak`, `audio.play`, `stream.scene.set`}. All
  three carry 0 literal secrets and pass `--check-config` without opening a
  transport. `pyproject.toml` ships the three new manifests so a clean
  install discovers 11 manifests. `docs/proxy-protocol.md` carries the R5
  addendum. `docs/README.md` carries (a) the phase 2 versioning section
  naming each allowlisted test and what replaced it and (b) the per-provider
  integration trial table with one row per provider — speech synthesis,
  transcription, playback command, capture command, scene provider, platform
  polls — each with the five fields commit, settings shape (no secret, no
  token), outcome (a run's result, or "not run" with the reason, e.g. the
  scene provider's dependency absent and port closed on the reference
  machine), limits, and date; `tests/test_phase2_trials.py` is the opt-in
  trial runner, skipped with a reason when its environment variables are
  unset. `core/` and `modules/` gain no model-name literal and `tests/` no
  positive-duration sleep.

Non-trivial requirement count: 7 (R1, R3, R4, R5, R6, R7, R8). R2 is a
declaration-and-proof requirement on phase 1 machinery and R9 is
configuration, packaging and documentation.

## Acceptance criteria

- AC1 (R1): With a scripted synthesis transport returning a 1.5 s WAV
  (48 000 data bytes at 16 kHz mono 16-bit) and a recording player runner
  that exits 0 after consuming its input, `audio.speak {text: "hello"}` under
  an applicable rule ends `success` with `playback: "completed"`,
  `duration_ms == 1500`, `played_ms == 1500`, exactly 1 synthesis request
  whose JSON body has `model`, `voice`, `input == "hello"` and
  `response_format == "wav"`, and exactly 1 player start that received all
  48 044 bytes; `audio.play {sound: "chime"}` on a configured 0.5 s clip ends
  `success` with `duration_ms == 500` and 0 synthesis requests.
- AC2 (R1): Each failure maps to its outcome with the stated effect count:
  transport exception → `error tts_unavailable`, 0 player starts; HTTP 503 →
  `error tts_failed` whose message contains `503` and none of the response
  body; no answer within `timeout_seconds` on the injected clock → `timeout`
  cause `synthesis`, 0 player starts; a 2xx body of 10 non-WAV bytes →
  `error invalid_audio`; a 25 s WAV with `max_speech_seconds: 20` → `error
  speech_too_long`, 0 player starts; a body of `max_audio_bytes + 1` bytes →
  `error audio_too_large`; a player exiting 1 → `error playback_failed`; a
  `sound` naming no clip → `error invalid_arguments`; a clip file removed
  after prepare → `error clip_unreadable`; `voice: "other"` outside
  `voices.allowed` → `refused voice_not_allowed` with 0 requests; `output:
  "other"` → `refused output_not_allowed` with 0 requests. No observation of
  these actions ever has status `external_unknown` across the whole test
  module.
- AC3 (R1): A recording player runner that accepts exactly 128 000 of the
  320 000 data bytes of a 10 s segment (16 kHz mono 16-bit, 32 000 B/s) and
  then stops reading: when the call deadline arrives (injected clock) the
  player is terminated, then killed after `stop_grace_seconds` when it
  ignores termination, and the call ends `timeout` cause `playback` with
  `played_ms == 4000` (128 000 ÷ 32 000 × 1 000); a cancellation at the
  same point ends `cancelled` with `played_ms == 4000`; in both cases the
  player runner records exactly 1 stop. A runner accepting 128 031 bytes
  yields `played_ms == 4000` too (rounded down); a runner that accepts
  everything and exits 1 yields `error playback_failed` with `played_ms ==
  10000`; a runner that cannot start yields `error playback_failed` with
  `played_ms == 0`; `played_ms` never exceeds `duration_ms`.
- AC4 (R1): Three concurrent `audio.speak` calls on one output with
  `max_waiters: 1`: the first plays, the second starts only after the first
  player exited (start order recorded by the runner), the third is `refused
  resource_busy` with 0 synthesis requests; two calls on two different
  outputs play concurrently (both started before either exited).
- AC5 (R1): At `prepare`, an output whose `argv[0]` is not executable leaves
  both actions unbound; a failing probe synthesis (transport error or
  timeout) leaves `audio.speak` unbound and `audio.play` bound, with
  `module.degraded` carrying `capabilities: ["audio.speak"]` and a reason
  containing neither the endpoint nor the key; with `synthesis.probe:
  false` and a usable output, 0 requests are sent at prepare, both actions
  are bound, no `module.degraded` is emitted, the same settings with
  `required: true` reach readiness, and a first `audio.speak` against a
  transport that raises ends `error tts_unavailable` with 0 player starts;
  the `api_key` value never appears in any trace or diagnostic (checked by
  substring over all emitted traces).
- AC6 (R2): With the real `audio_output` module and scripted seams, a brain
  configured `delivery: {mode: fixed, actions: [{chat.write, text},
  {audio.speak, text}]}` delivers one final answer with `deliveries ==
  [{chat.write, text: true, success}, {audio.speak, text: true, success}]`,
  the synthesis request `input` equal to the final text, and exactly 1
  `chat.write` send; `[{chat.write, text}, {audio.play, none, arguments:
  {sound: chime}}]` yields `audio.play` `text: false` with the clip played;
  `[{stream.scene.set, none, arguments: {scene: Talking}}]` with the
  scripted scene provider yields one set command and `delivery == success`.
- AC7 (R2): `mode: modules` with `audio_output`, `stream_control` (scripted
  scenes) and the platform enabled and `preference: [chat.write,
  audio.speak]` resolves `[chat.write, audio.speak, audio.play,
  stream.scene.set]` (preferred first, then catalog order) and traces
  `brain.delivery.resolved`; with `audio_output` not ready (failed probe) the
  `audio.speak` entry records `refused` code `provider_not_ready`, the other
  entries keep their outcomes and conversation memory is not written (phase
  1 rule); an `external_unknown` on `audio.speak` across a dropped proxy is
  never memorised as a confirmed send. The diff of this phase contains no
  hunk in the brain's delivery resolution or turn loop attributable to R2
  (checked by the gate review over `git diff <base>..HEAD -- modules/brain/`;
  R4's adapter hunks are the only brain change).
- AC8 (R3): With an injected source producing 5 s of valid WAV (160 044
  bytes), `audio.capture {seconds: 3}` under an applicable rule ends
  `success` with one `audio_ref` part whose `size == 96 044`, `duration_ms ==
  3000`, `sample_rate_hz == 16000`, `channels == 1`, `content_type ==
  "audio/wav"`, an attachment of exactly 96 044 bytes leased to the call's
  run, `captured_at` set from the injected clock and `transcription_status
  == "disabled"`; the stored bytes start with `RIFF`/`WAVE` and their data
  chunk holds 96 000 bytes.
- AC9 (R3): A source producing 8 000 Hz audio, stereo audio, 8-bit audio or
  20 non-WAV bytes yields `error invalid_result` with 0 attachments stored;
  with the default `max_seconds: 30`, `seconds: 40` is `error
  invalid_arguments` before any process is spawned; with `max_seconds: 40`
  and `max_bytes: 1_048_576`, a valid 40 s source (1 280 044 bytes) is
  `error attachment_refused` with 0 bytes stored; with the defaults and
  `seconds: 30`, the 960 044-byte segment is stored and `size == 960_044`.
- AC10 (R3): With 4 s left before the call deadline and `grace_seconds: 2`,
  `seconds: 3` is `error capture_too_long` with 0 processes spawned; a
  recorder that has emitted nothing when the deadline arrives is killed
  (runner records 1 kill) and the call is `error capture_timed_out` with 0
  attachments; a cancellation mid-capture kills it and ends `cancelled`
  with 0 attachments; after the run's terminal record every attachment of
  the run is gone from the store and a lease that outlived a crashed run is
  reaped by the TTL on the injected clock.
- AC11 (R3): With `transcription.enabled: true` and a scripted transport
